WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.569
-You were just in Pittsburgh
for Steel City Con.

00:00:03.569 --> 00:00:04.903
How does it feel --

00:00:04.903 --> 00:00:07.039
Much has been written
about the fact that, you know,

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:08.842
Star Trek was not
anywhere near as popular

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.142
when it was airing as it is now.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:11.411
Is it still surreal to you

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.179
that fans are so deeply
connected to that show?

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.881
-It's 53 years old.

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:18.751
We went on the air in 1966,
canceled in 1969,

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:23.289
exactly 50 years ago,
and here we are, 53 years later,

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:27.927
with another new Star Trek
spin-off with Patrick Stewart

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:32.732
and another movie being talked
about by -- with Tarentino.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:35.869
I mean,
it's an amazing phenomenon.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:39.739
-And people must still be
so happy to see you

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.676
when they go to something
like Steel City Con?

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:46.479
-I greet my fans like this.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:48.981
-You know how to please them.
So...

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:52.619
-We have lived much longer
than we expected

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:55.788
and prospered
in so many wondrous ways,

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.223
like all these fans.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:58.858
-That's fantastic.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.460
This show, "Terror: Infamy" --

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.195
this is much more serious
subject matter, and, uh,

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:07.032
it's based on the internment
of Japanese-Americans.

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:09.002
Uh, there's a supernatural
element to it,

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:10.903
but this is a very real
issue for you --

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.438
not just being
a Japanese-American,

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:15.175
but you were interned as a child
from the ages five to eight.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:18.344
Is that right?
-I'd just turned five years old.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.179
A few weeks after that,

00:01:20.179 --> 00:01:23.216
my parents got me up
very early one morning,

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:27.520
dressed us hurriedly up --
my brother a year younger

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.591
and my baby sister
still a baby, an infant --

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:35.194
and, uh, my father
said to my brother and me,

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:37.163
"Wait here in the living room

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:39.698
while we do some
last-minute packing."

00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:42.402
So the two of us were
just looking out the --

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:44.604
gazing out the front window,

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:49.242
and suddenly we saw two soldiers
marching up our driveway

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:52.978
carrying rifles
with shiny bayonets on them.

00:01:52.978 --> 00:01:54.480
They stomped up the porch

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.985
and, with their fists,
began pounding on the door.

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:01.153
I -- the way I remember it,

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:03.789
the whole house
seemed to tremble.

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:06.960
And my father came out
and answered the door,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.861
and, literally at gunpoint,

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:11.330
we were ordered
out of our house.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:15.668
And so my father gave my brother
and me little packages to carry.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.804
He hefted two heavy suitcases,

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:20.840
and we followed him
out onto the driveway

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.709
and waited for our mother
to come out.

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:27.947
When she came out, she had
our baby sister in one arm

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.116
a heavy duffel bag in the other,

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:32.952
and tears were streaming
down her cheeks.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:36.522
It is a picture
that's burnt into my memory.

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:38.658
-What did you think
was happening

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.527
when being that young
in a situation like that?

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:43.695
What were your memories
at the time of what this was

00:02:43.695 --> 00:02:45.398
and why it was happening?

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.634
-That morning
was a terrifying morning,

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.670
but then we were taken --

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.107
you know, the camps
were just being built,

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:57.543
so they took us
to Santa Anita Race Track,

00:02:57.543 --> 00:02:59.211
a nearby race track.

00:02:59.211 --> 00:03:03.649
We were herded over with other
Japanese-American families

00:03:03.649 --> 00:03:07.319
to the stable area
and assigned a horse stall

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:08.921
for us to sleep in.

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:12.992
From a two-bedroom home,
front yard, backyard,

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.227
on Garnet Street in LA,

00:03:15.227 --> 00:03:16.495
to a horse stall.

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:18.097
For my parents,

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:22.702
it was a degrading, humiliating,
painful experience

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:26.939
to take their three children
into that smelly horse stall,

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:28.307
still pungent with the --

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:31.243
and I still remember that smell,
but, for me,

00:03:31.243 --> 00:03:33.812
I thought it was fun to sleep
where the horsies sleep.

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:34.948
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:37.450
-You know, horsies slept here.
-Sure.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.851
-I can smell them.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:43.289
-Now, how surreal was it
for you to return to a set

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.659
that was built
to evoke this time

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.796
and having such specific
memories of it?

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:51.029
I mean, obviously,
you're coming back

00:03:51.029 --> 00:03:52.197
in a much better
perspective on it,

00:03:52.197 --> 00:03:54.067
having the control
of telling this story,

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:55.968
but was it hard for you
to walk onto a set

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.136
and see those camps again?

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:59.505
-Remember,
I was a five-year-old kid,

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:06.712
and they built
on a 6.5-acre plot of land

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:10.850
an exact replica
of the internment camps.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.887
The barracks were exactly
the way I remembered it.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:18.457
There was a little crawlspace
down below,

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:25.131
and I recognized that
because we adopted a black dog,

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:26.565
cute little dog.

00:04:26.565 --> 00:04:27.934
We named him Blackie.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:30.202
And whenever something
scared him --

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:32.605
you know, when gunfire
was going off --

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:37.443
he got scared, and he would
crawl under the crawlspace,

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.946
and we crawled in after him,

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:42.981
so, you know,
I remembered the details.

00:04:42.981 --> 00:04:46.619
The -- The set designers
did tremendous research.

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:51.591
The strips of wood
that held the tar paper on

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.060
were exactly
the same dimensions,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:05:00.099
so it was, to me,
a kind of a nostalgic return

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:04.837
because of my childhood
experience, but, as a teenager,

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:08.140
I learned a lot more
about the reality --

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:13.112
the harrowing experience that it
was for my parents, and so, uh,

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:17.984
with both the adult knowledge
and the memory of a child,

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:20.386
it was eerie,
kind of, the feeling.

